solution:
The language is {0,1}*1{0,1}{0,1}. An NFA can guess which 1 will be third from last, then require exactly two more symbols before acceptance. Use states q0,q1,q2,q3; q0 is initial and q3 is the only accepting state. Missing transitions go to the empty set.

Transition table:
State | on 0 | on 1
q0    | {q0} | {q0,q1}
q1    | {q2} | {q2}
q2    | {q3} | {q3}
q3    | {}   | {}

Equivalently, q0 has a loop labeled 0,1 and an additional edge labeled 1 to q1; q1→q2 and q2→q3 are labeled 0,1. A successful path stays at q0 through an arbitrary prefix, takes the guessing edge on a 1, and reads exactly two additional symbols. It ends in q3 precisely for the required strings.

For a DFA upper bound, store the most recent three bits, padding on the left with zeros before three bits have arrived. Use the eight states abc∈{0,1}^3, initial state 000, transition δ(abc,d)=bcd, and accepting states 100,101,110,111. After at least three inputs the first stored bit is exactly third from last; shorter strings remain nonaccepting because their padded first bit is zero. Every state is reachable by reading its three-bit label.

For minimality, take two different length-three strings x and y, differing at position r counted from the left, with 1≤r≤3. Append 0^{r−1} to both. In each resulting string, the third symbol from the right is precisely the old position r, so exactly one extension is accepted. Thus all eight three-bit strings have pairwise distinct right languages. By Myhill–Nerode, every DFA needs at least eight states. The construction meets this bound, proving that its minimized size is exactly 8.

First distinguish the function’s returned numerical value from its running time. In one outer iteration the inner loop increments C exactly F(n−1) times, so C=F(n−1). Starting from F1=1, the outer loop multiplies by that same value n times. Hence for n>1 the returned value is F(n)=∏[i=1..n] F(n−1)=[F(n−1)]^n. The base is F(1)=3 if the base-case assignment is returned without being overwritten.

A block-explicit rendition of this intended reading is:
function F(n):
  if n=1 then return 3;
  F1:=1;
  for i:=1 to n do begin
    C:=0;
    for j:=1 to F(n−1) do C:=C+1;
    F1:=F1*C
  end;
  return F1.
This explains why neither n·F(n−1) (a sum) nor a runtime recurrence answers the value question. For instance F(2)=3²=9 and F(3)=9³=729.

Source qualification: both reproductions place a final F=F1 after the if/else loop without clearly enclosing it in the else branch. Under a literal Pascal-like reading with that assignment executed unconditionally, n=1 first sets F=3 but then overwrites it with the initial F1=1. The recurrence becomes F(1)=1, F(n)=F(n−1)^n and all values are 1. The intended nontrivial recurrence therefore requires treating the final assignment as part of the else computation or returning immediately from the n=1 case. This control-flow assumption is explicit rather than silently repaired.

In an undirected DFS tree, every non-tree edge joins an ancestor and a descendant. To see why, suppose an edge joined distinct DFS subtrees: while its first endpoint’s subtree was explored, the still-undiscovered other endpoint would be reached by that edge and placed in the same subtree, a contradiction. Thus the tree path between u and v is a single upward parent chain from the descendant. Which endpoint is deeper is not given, so climbing just one chosen endpoint can waste time or stick at the root.

Climb both parent chains in lockstep, saving both traversals, and stop as soon as one reaches the other original endpoint:
procedure PrintCycle(u,v);
begin
  a:=u; b:=v; lenA:=1; lenB:=1;
  pathA[1]:=u; pathB[1]:=v;
  while (a<>v) and (b<>u) do begin
    a:=P[a]; lenA:=lenA+1; pathA[lenA]:=a;
    b:=P[b]; lenB:=lenB+1; pathB[lenB]:=b
  end;
  if a=v then begin
    for k:=1 to lenA do write(pathA[k]);
    write(u)
  end else begin
    for k:=1 to lenB do write(pathB[k]);
    write(v)
  end
end.
Assume arrays can hold the tree height plus one and append/write are constant-time operations. Storage can instead be growable linked lists with constant-time append. Arrays need no initial clearing; only positions written above are read.

Let the ancestor–descendant distance be d. The descendant walk reaches the ancestor after exactly d rounds, even if the other walk has already reached the root and is repeating its self-parent. The algorithm performs at most 2d parent moves and prints d+1 distinct cycle vertices plus the repeated start: Θ(d+1), proportional to the cycle’s d+1 edges, with O(d+1) temporary storage. For example a parent chain 7→5→2 and new edge (2,7) prints 7,5,2,7.

No full-tree traversal, depth computation or global visited-array clearing is required. The argument assumes the usual simple undirected graph; a self-loop can be handled immediately by printing u,u. For an arbitrary spanning tree the ancestor property is false, so this algorithm is specifically justified by the DFS premise.

Use two length-n arrays: dense[1..m] stores precisely the current set in arbitrary order; pos[x] is x’s dense position if present, otherwise 0. The invariant is pos[dense[j]]=j for 1≤j≤m, and pos[x]=0 exactly for absent elements. Initialize m:=0 and pos[1..n]:=0, which takes Θ(n) time once. dense need not be initialized because only its active prefix is read. Space is Θ(n). Array indexing, integer comparison and assignment are constant-time in the word-RAM model, with words large enough for 0..n. Operations assume 1≤x≤n or reject out-of-range x before indexing.

Pictorial example, n=7 and S={2,5,7}:
dense positions: 1  2  3 | 4 5 6 7
dense values:    2  5  7 | unused
pos index:      1  2  3  4  5  6  7
pos value:      0  1  0  0  2  0  3
m=3; arrows are 2→1, 5→2, 7→3. A plain Boolean array gives easy membership but could require Θ(n) scanning to FIND-ONE; the dense prefix supplies an immediate element. Set order is deliberately not preserved, which enables constant-time deletion.

Routine:
DELETE(x):
  require 1<=x<=n;
  k:=pos[x];
  if k=0 then return;
  y:=dense[m];
  dense[k]:=y;
  pos[y]:=k;
  m:=m-1;
  pos[x]:=0.
The position array directly locates x, avoiding a search. Moving the final member into x’s slot avoids shifting the remaining members. Updating pos[y] repairs the one inverse link affected by the move. The shortened prefix contains all old members except x, and clearing pos[x] makes future membership queries false.

Example DELETE(5) on dense [2,5,7] moves 7 from position 3 to position 2. The new active prefix is [2,7], m=2, pos[7]=2 and pos[5]=0. The old dense[3] value is irrelevant because it lies outside the prefix. If x already is the final element, then y=x; the self-assignment is harmless and the last assignment pos[x]:=0 is essential. The same code safely empties a singleton set. Deleting an absent element leaves everything unchanged. Only a fixed number of assignments and lookups are performed, proving worst-case O(1).

The separate local variable receives the initial value and is copied back on return, so the intended mechanism is call by value-result, also called copy-in/copy-out. It is not call by reference, which uses the actual storage throughout execution, nor call by value, which never copies a final value back. Call by name re-evaluates an actual-argument expression in its caller environment on each use rather than using one private copied value.

The proposed copy-back re-evaluates A[i]. If the caller’s index changes during execution, it can select a different element from the one used at entry. For example initially i=2 and A[2]=10. Suppose the body changes the caller-visible index to 3 through a global reference and leaves the formal’s local value as 99. Re-evaluating A[i] on exit writes A[3]=99 instead of copying back into A[2]. An implementation must preserve the original actual location under the usual location-fixed value-result convention.

Small correction:
p:=address_of(A[i]);   {evaluate actual location in caller environment once}
z:=value_at(p);       {copy in}
execute P body with every occurrence of its formal bound to z;
value_at(p):=z;       {copy out to saved location}
If A is known not to change identity or base address, saving k:=i and using A[k] for both transfers is sufficient; saving the address is the general correction. Normal return is assumed, and with one parameter there is no multiple-copy-back ordering issue.

There is also a naming issue in the printed wording: P’s formal is named i, while the actual A[i] contains the caller’s i. They are different bindings. The body must substitute z for the formal, not literally search for the caller expression A[i] in the body. Merely assigning the formal does not itself change the caller’s separate index; the failure requires that the caller-visible index or array location can otherwise change. If that cannot happen, the original sequence happens to produce the right result.

