An array A[1..n], n≥1, contains positive integers, and M>0. Complete the boxes in this program so it prints the shortest nonempty contiguous-subarray length having sum at least M, or n+1 if none exists. The program uses one-based array indexing:
1 begin
2   i:=1; j:=1;
3   sum:=[B3];
4   min:=n; finish:=false;
5   while not finish do
6     if [B6] then
7       if j=n then finish:=true
8       else
9         begin
10          j:=j+1;
11          sum:=[B11]
12        end
13    else
14      begin
15        if (j-i)<min then min:=j-i;
16        sum:=sum-A[i];
17        i:=i+1
18      end;
19  writeln(min+1)
20 end

The following C-like code removes duplicate values from a sorted singly linked list. Node has int val and Node *next. Assume the input is acyclic and contains n≥2 nodes; j points to a separate valid integer. Pointer-member syntax is normalized from the reproduced typesetting.
Node *remove_duplicates(Node *head,int *j) {
  Node *t1,*t2;
  *j=0;
  t1=head;
  if(t1!=NULL) t2=t1->next;
  else return head;
  *j=1;
  if(t2==NULL) return head;
  while(t2!=NULL) {
    if(t1->val!=t2->val) {             /* S1 */
      (*j)++; t1->next=t2; t1=t2;      /* S2 */
    }
    t2=t2->next;
  }
  t1->next=NULL;
  return head;
}
How many times is the comparison S1 evaluated?

