Consider this Pascal nesting and call structure, with no calls other than those shown. Main declares X; A, nested in Main, declares Y; B, nested in A, declares Z; C, nested in B, also declares Z. Main calls A; A calls B; B calls C and, after C returns, calls A. C contains no procedure calls. At an instant there are five activation records on the runtime stack. Show its top, static and dynamic links, and local variables. Source skeleton:
program Main; var X: integer;
  procedure A; var Y: integer;
    procedure B; var Z: integer;
      procedure C; var Z: integer;
      begin {no procedure calls} end;
    begin C; A end;
  begin B end;
begin A end.
Ellipses in the source allow other non-call work; the skeleton preserves every shown declaration and call.

solution:
S1 must finish first. Afterward S3 can run independently of the chain S2 then S4. S5 must wait for both branches. A structured program is:

begin
  S1;
  parbegin
    begin
      S2;
      S4;
    end
    S3;
  parend;
  S5;
end

Here parbegin starts its two child statements concurrently, and parend joins them. The first child is a sequential block, so S2 precedes S4. The second child is S3. The final join guarantees both S3 and S4 finish before S5. Edges S1→S4 and S1→S5 are already implied by the paths through S2/S4, but are still satisfied. There is no imposed order between S3 and S2 or between S3 and S4, so the program preserves the graph's available parallelism rather than unnecessarily serializing it.

