solution:
Use the shared lock variable mutex, initially zero:
void enter_cs(void) { while (TEST_AND_SET(&mutex)) { } }
void leave_cs(void) { mutex = 0; }
TEST_AND_SET atomically returns the old value and writes one. Exactly one contender observing the unlocked zero receives zero and leaves the loop; later contenders receive one and keep spinning. Releasing the lock restores zero for a future acquisition. This is pseudocode for the given atomic primitive and a shared lock, not a claim that an ordinary non-atomic C integer and a handwritten function implement modern concurrent-C synchronization. The source's hyphenated function names are rendered with underscores to make the names valid C identifiers.

Complete the source placeholders with:
p1: wait(b_empty);
p2: signal(b_full);
c1: wait(b_full);
c2: signal(b_empty);
The producer reserves an empty slot before writing buf[first] and advancing first, then announces a full slot. The consumer reserves a full slot before reading buf[last] and advancing last, then announces an empty slot. With b_empty initially BUFSIZE=100 and b_full initially zero, counting semaphores prevent overrun and underrun. Only one producer updates first and only one consumer updates last, so no additional mutex is needed for this single-producer/single-consumer algorithm under the usual atomic semaphore and memory-visibility assumptions. A semaphore count can exclude a reserved slot temporarily while its operation is in progress; b_empty+b_full need not equal BUFSIZE at every instruction boundary.

